WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday! It's

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:03.629
Question Tuesday, the day that I answer real

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:05.100
questions from real Nerdfighters. Let's

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.569
get right to it! "Do rich people have

00:00:06.569 --> 00:00:08.309
better Wi-Fi, or does Beyonce have to

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:10.349
unplug the router sometimes?" Interesting

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:12.450
fact: money can buy you almost anything,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.549
but it cannot buy you freedom from

00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:16.680
Comcast. I'm pretty sure Beyonce has to

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.119
reset her router just like the rest of

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:19.500
us. "How do I cope with the fact that

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.210
there's no real meaning in life?"

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.590
Oh man, I wish we could just go back to

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.810
Beyonce questions. So, listen, maybe meaning

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.849
in life is constructed by us rather than

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:29.010
derived from some objective truth, but

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.990
that doesn't make meaning not real.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:32.430
Okay, so, you should always be suspicious of

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.899
people who use metaphor to approach

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:35.730
questions of meaning, however, imagine

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.870
you're walking through a forest one

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.340
night, and you come to a clearing and you

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.559
find an eternal fire. It's the perfectly

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:42.360
sized fire and it doesn't need anything

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.739
from you. You don't have to put wood in

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:45.809
it, it just burns and burns. And you can

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:47.129
spend your whole life seeing by its

00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:49.260
light, and being warmed by it, and so on. I

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.760
think that's an awesome fire and if you find

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.320
one you should be grateful, but imagine

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.820
that you don't. Imagine on your travels

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.739
you never come across an eternal fire. In

00:00:55.739 --> 00:00:57.329
that case, you have to build a fire and

00:00:57.329 --> 00:00:59.100
then find fuel to keep it going and

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.780
sometimes it'll go out and you've got to

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.489
start a new fire. Now, in some ways that's

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:03.809
worse, but in some ways it's better

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:05.640
because unlike the eternal fire, you

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.369
understand how and why it works. Point

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:09.689
being, I would argue the light and heat

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:11.880
from both fires are equally real. Alright,

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.140
that's all I got, no more questions about

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.070
the meaning of life.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.119
"Will you come to my wedding in September?

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:16.650
My partner is obsessed with you

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.150
and your wife." So, when Sarah and I got

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.560
married we invited a somewhat famous

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.270
person, who shall remain nameless, to our

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.979
wedding. He wasn't like a close friend,

00:01:22.979 --> 00:01:24.479
but he was someone who'd had an important

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:25.860
role in getting us together. And we

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:26.909
thought it would be nice to invite him.

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:28.080
So anyway, a couple weeks after the

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.460
invitations come out, I get an email

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.659
and it begins,

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:32.640
"Of course I cannot come to your wedding"

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.380
What do you mean "of course"?! Is there a less

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.210
necessary "of course" in the history of

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.950
the English language? Would not "I cannot

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.659
come to your wedding" have sufficed?

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:41.369
Anyway, he did buy us some wine glasses off

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:43.079
our registry, which we still have, so

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:43.829
thank you.

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:45.360
Right, but to your question, of course we

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.070
cannot come to your wedding. I'm kidding!

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.750
If it's in indianapolis and there's an open

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.340
bar, we'll be there. "Favorite fictional

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.140
depiction of time travel?" I like your

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.610
implication that there are non fictional

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.439
depictions of time-travel. Probably, for

00:01:55.439 --> 00:01:57.390
me, Back to the Future, which has had kind of

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.430
an outsized influence on my life. "Is there

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.689
going to be another season of Crash

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:02.250
Course: Literature?" Yeah, probably. What

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.810
should we read? "I want to know everything

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.939
about your new book." But sadly, I don't

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:07.590
know everything about it, because it's

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.819
not finished, yet.

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:10.140
"Are you tired of people asking about your new

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.849
book?" No, it's nice that people care, I

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:13.260
just wish I had something more to tell

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.670
them. "What's your favorite river?"

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:15.900
Definitely the White River, here in

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.280
Indianapolis. Even though Indianapolis

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.870
is a city of a million people, the river

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.060
is really beautiful and mostly deserted.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.889
Like, you can kayak for hours without

00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:23.909
seeing anyone.

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:25.200
And the reason it's so beautiful is that

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.840
it's basically a heavily polluted open

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.150
sewer, and by basically I mean it is

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.519
those things. So it seems natural and

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:34.769
pristine precisely because it is so

00:02:34.769 --> 00:02:37.019
unnatural and polluted. It's just a very

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:38.879
metaphorically resonant river. "Favorite

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:40.260
online server to store and organize

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.340
information?"

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.200
Nice try, hackers, but not this time. "Do

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.389
you like the number 53?" Mm..It's probably in my

00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:47.849
top half of 2-digit numbers. 43 is the

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:50.370
best, though. "Do you ever go by any nicknames?"

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.359
Well, some of my friends from high school

00:02:51.359 --> 00:02:53.340
still call me Kuffs, because I once said that

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.049
Christian Slater never made a bad movie.

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:57.239
"Do thoughts mean consciousness?" So, like,

00:02:57.239 --> 00:02:59.549
I spend probably the majority of my free

00:02:59.549 --> 00:03:01.049
time thinking about whether I am

00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:03.269
actually comprised of my thoughts, and to

00:03:03.269 --> 00:03:04.919
what extent the thoughts that are called

00:03:04.919 --> 00:03:06.989
mine actually belong to me, but I haven't

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:08.969
come to any meaningful conclusions.

00:03:08.969 --> 00:03:10.739
"Why've people become so obsessed with being

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:12.629
right, rather than developing richer ways

00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:14.129
of thinking?" I don't know. I'm trying to

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:15.479
practice saying "I don't know" more,

00:03:15.479 --> 00:03:17.370
because, it seems to me, a really

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.979
underutilized phrase. "Hey, what's in the boxes

00:03:19.979 --> 00:03:20.790
behind you?"

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:22.049
I don't know. "When's your new book going to

00:03:22.049 --> 00:03:23.669
come out?" I don't know. "If your greatest

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:25.379
hope and your greatest fear challenged each

00:03:25.379 --> 00:03:27.269
other to a duel, who would win?"

00:03:27.269 --> 00:03:29.069
So, who would win a duel between absolute

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:31.470
oblivion and a cure for malaria?

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.180
I don't know! Hank, this "I don't know" stuff

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.340
is amazing. The life-changing magic of "I

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.290
don't know"! Hank, I will see you on

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.790
Friday. Actually, I don't know for sure

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:40.109
that I'm going to see you on Friday, but

00:03:40.109 --> 00:03:42.799
I hope I do.

